### F7 — Dense vs sparse core-distance semantics diverge on self-distance handling [out-of-theme? no, correctness]
severity: low
evidence: sklearn/cluster/_hdbscan/_reachability.pyx:132-137 computes core distance from the full row of `distance_matrix` (dense case includes the diagonal 0 = self-distance), while sklearn/cluster/_hdbscan/_reachability.pyx:194-198 computes it from `data[indptr[i]:indptr[i+1]]` which contains only explicit non-zero entries — so if the sparse matrix has been `eliminate_zeros()`d (standard practice), the self-entry is absent from `row_data`, giving `core_distances[i]` = the (further_neighbor_idx)-th smallest **excluding** self. Dense returns the distance to the `further_neighbor_idx`-th nearest neighbor counting self as position 0; sparse returns the distance to the `further_neighbor_idx`-th nearest neighbor among stored entries (self excluded).
scenario: "user passes a precomputed sparse CSR distance matrix with implicit zero diagonal → sparse core distance is effectively 'kth' rather than '(k-1)th' nearest neighbor, so clustering result diverges from what dense would yield for the same values"
contract: align semantics — either strip the diagonal in the dense case before selecting `further_neighbor_idx`, or account for the missing diagonal by shifting the sparse index (e.g., use `further_neighbor_idx - 1` when diagonal absent).
